## **Approach 2: Optimized - Sliding Window with HashMap**

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    /**
     * APPROACH: SLIDING WINDOW WITH FREQUENCY COUNT (HashMap)
     * 
     * Algorithm:
     * 1. Create frequency map for pattern p
     * 2. Create frequency map for first window in s
     * 3. Compare both maps - if equal, found anagram at index 0
     * 4. Slide the window:
     *    - Add the new character entering the window (increment count)
     *    - Remove the character leaving the window (decrement count)
     *    - Compare frequency maps after each slide
     * 5. Collect all indices where maps match
     * 
     * Key Insight:
     *   - Two strings are anagrams if they have same character frequencies
     *   - Instead of sorting every window, maintain frequency counts
     *   - Update counts incrementally as window slides (O(1) per slide)
     * 
     * Time Complexity: O(n)
     *   - Single pass through string s
     *   - Map comparison is O(26) = O(1) for lowercase letters
     * 
     * Space Complexity: O(1)
     *   - Maps contain at most 26 keys (lowercase letters)
     *   - Constant space regardless of input size
     * 
     * Trade-offs:
     *   - Uses extra space for HashMaps
     *   - Much faster than brute force
     *   - Works for any character set (not just lowercase)
     */
    public List<Integer> findAnagrams(String s, String p) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        
        // Edge case: if p is longer than s, no anagrams possible
        if (p.length() > s.length()) {
            return result;
        }
        
        // Step 1: Build frequency map for pattern p
        Map<Character, Integer> pCount = new HashMap<>();
        for (char c : p.toCharArray()) {
            pCount.put(c, pCount.getOrDefault(c, 0) + 1);
        }
        
        // Step 2: Build frequency map for sliding window
        Map<Character, Integer> windowCount = new HashMap<>();
        
        int windowSize = p.length();
        
        // Step 3: Build the first window (indices 0 to windowSize-1)
        for (int i = 0; i < windowSize; i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);
            windowCount.put(c, windowCount.getOrDefault(c, 0) + 1);
        }
        
        // Step 4: Check if first window is an anagram
        if (windowCount.equals(pCount)) {
            result.add(0);
        }
        
        // Step 5: Slide the window through the rest of string s
        for (int i = windowSize; i < s.length(); i++) {
            // Add new character entering the window (right side)
            char newChar = s.charAt(i);
            windowCount.put(newChar, windowCount.getOrDefault(newChar, 0) + 1);
            
            // Remove old character leaving the window (left side)
            char oldChar = s.charAt(i - windowSize);
            windowCount.put(oldChar, windowCount.get(oldChar) - 1);
            
            // Clean up: remove character from map if count becomes 0
            // This ensures accurate map comparison
            if (windowCount.get(oldChar) == 0) {
                windowCount.remove(oldChar);
            }
            
            // Check if current window is an anagram of p
            if (windowCount.equals(pCount)) {
                // Starting index of current window
                result.add(i - windowSize + 1);
            }
        }
        
        return result;
    }
}
