## **Approach 3: Further Optimized - Sliding Window with Array**

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    /**
     * APPROACH: SLIDING WINDOW WITH FREQUENCY COUNT (Array)
     * 
     * Algorithm:
     * Same as Approach 2, but uses int[26] arrays instead of HashMap
     * 
     * Optimization:
     *   - For lowercase letters only, we can use array indexing
     *   - Index calculation: char - 'a' gives index 0-25
     *   - Array comparison is slightly faster than HashMap comparison
     *   - No hash function overhead
     * 
     * Time Complexity: O(n)
     *   - Single pass through string s
     *   - Array comparison is O(26) = O(1)
     * 
     * Space Complexity: O(1)
     *   - Fixed array of size 26
     * 
     * Trade-offs:
     *   - Fastest implementation
     *   - Only works for lowercase letters (26 characters)
     *   - If input has uppercase or special chars, use HashMap approach
     *   - Most commonly used in competitive programming
     */
    public List<Integer> findAnagrams(String s, String p) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        
        // Edge case: if p is longer than s, no anagrams possible
        if (p.length() > s.length()) {
            return result;
        }
        
        // Step 1: Create frequency arrays
        // Index 0 represents 'a', index 1 represents 'b', ..., index 25 represents 'z'
        int[] pCount = new int[26];
        int[] windowCount = new int[26];
        
        // Step 2: Build frequency array for pattern p
        for (char c : p.toCharArray()) {
            pCount[c - 'a']++;  // Convert char to index: 'a'->0, 'b'->1, etc.
        }
        
        int windowSize = p.length();
        
        // Step 3: Build frequency array for first window
        for (int i = 0; i < windowSize; i++) {
            windowCount[s.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
        }
        
        // Step 4: Check if first window is an anagram
        if (Arrays.equals(pCount, windowCount)) {
            result.add(0);
        }
        
        // Step 5: Slide the window through rest of string s
        for (int i = windowSize; i < s.length(); i++) {
            // Add new character entering the window (right side)
            windowCount[s.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
            
            // Remove old character leaving the window (left side)
            windowCount[s.charAt(i - windowSize) - 'a']--;
            
            // Check if current window matches pattern p
            if (Arrays.equals(pCount, windowCount)) {
                // Starting index of current window
                result.add(i - windowSize + 1);
            }
        }
        
        return result;
    }
}
